# ${SRC_DIR}/

Application code for ${PROJECT_NAME}. Stamped on ${TIMESTAMP}.

## Layout

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| [`server.ts`](server.ts) | HTTP server bootstrap — listens, handles graceful shutdown. |
| [`app.ts`](app.ts) | Express composition only. No business logic. |
| [`config/`](config/) | Environment loader, logger, DB clients. |
| [`middlewares/`](middlewares/) | Cross-cutting request middleware. |
| [`models/`](models/) | Data models — one file per collection or table. |
| [`lib/`](lib/) | Framework-agnostic primitives. No imports from `middlewares/`, `models/`, or `api/`. |
| [`api/${API_VERSION}/<feature>/`](api/${API_VERSION}/) | Per-feature folders — see the feature-folder rule below. |
| [`types/`](types/) | Shared type declarations and module augmentations. |
| [`tests/`](tests/) | Unit + integration tests + fixtures. |

## The strict 4-subfolder feature rule

Every feature under `api/${API_VERSION}/<name>/` has **exactly these four subfolders**:

| Subfolder | What lives here | Naming suffix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `routes/` | Route registration (`router.get('/x', handler)`) | `*.routes.ts` |
| `controllers/` | Thin handlers — parse input, call service, format response | `*.controller.ts` |
| `services/` | Business logic — pure functions where possible | `*.service.ts` |
| `types/` | Types and interfaces local to the feature | `*.types.ts` |

No file lives at the feature root. The feature's own `README.md` is the only
top-level file inside a feature folder.

## Naming convention

Content must be inferable from the suffix. Examples:

- `users.routes.ts` — route registration for the users feature.
- `users.controller.ts` — request handlers (TwiML / JSON only, no business logic).
- `users.service.ts` — business logic.
- `users.types.ts` — request/response and domain types.
- `user.model.ts` — Mongoose / pg row type (lives in `${SRC_DIR}/models/`, not the feature folder).
- `users.spec.ts` / `users.test.ts` — tests (live in `${SRC_DIR}/tests/`).

## File size

Soft cap: **300–500 lines**. Run `npm run lint:size` to surface offenders. The
check is warning-only.
